gnome-system-monitor-rh (50.0+rh.2) noble; urgency=medium

  * Packaging: the health sampler's user unit is installed but no longer
    enabled for every user. Version 50.0+rh.1 linked it into
    /etc/systemd/user/default.target.wants, so the Health tab's off switch
    could not keep it off. Upgrading from an earlier version removes that
    system-wide link and the state deb-systemd-helper kept for it; a unit a
    user enabled in their own home is left alone, and fresh installs enable
    nothing.
  * Processes: CPU % now covers every thread of a process. 50.0+rh.1 took
    it from /proc/PID/schedstat, which is the first thread's alone, so
    browsers, editors and JVMs showed near 0 %. It is now utime + stime of
    /proc/PID/stat over the measured interval.
  * Health: the wear sampler reads the per-process and per-cgroup write
    counters every 15 seconds as before, but writes its record every 10
    minutes and when it stops, about 200 KiB an hour instead of about
    5.5 MB. Drive health is read every 10 minutes, as often as udisks
    refreshes it. The writer list says when the record was last written.
  * Collector: a table of workload groups (class rows, then units,
    applications, sessions and agent sessions) with memory, tasks, CPU over
    the interval and a residue that adds up to the kernel's user-space
    total; not yet shown in the window.

 -- Pranav <pranavrajput321@gmail.com>  Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:03:13 +0800

gnome-system-monitor-rh (50.0+rh.1) noble; urgency=medium

  * First packaged release of the fork, based on GNOME System Monitor 50.0.
    Replaces the distribution's gnome-system-monitor (Provides, Conflicts and
    Replaces), so the fork's menu entry, D-Bus name and settings are the ones
    the distribution package used.
  * Memory: the Resources tab reads /proc/meminfo directly and shows
    Available, RAM-backed files (tmpfs and shared memory, labelled as not
    reclaimable without swap) and File cache apart, so tmpfs no longer
    counts as reclaimable cache. The process column formerly titled "Memory"
    is anonymous resident memory and is now titled "Private (anon)".
  * Disk I/O: values the kernel does not let the user read are shown as "—"
    instead of 0, the totals row says how many rows were readable, and rates
    use the measured interval.
  * GPU: NVIDIA per-process GPU memory works (the nvidia-smi query was
    invalid and always showed 0); per-process GPU utilisation is "—" until a
    source for it is used. GPU utilisation and memory graphs and columns are
    new and appear only when nvidia-smi is found.
  * CPU: labels follow the real topology (P-core threads P1a/P1b, E-cores
    E1.., or "CPU n" when the kernel does not publish it). Hybrid Intel parts
    were mislabelled before.
  * Resources tab: a Pressure line under CPU, memory and disk shows the share
    of time tasks were stalled on that resource, from /proc/pressure.
  * Disk: the disk graph reads /proc/diskstats once per refresh and counts
    whole physical disks only (not partitions, loop, zram, dm or md); the
    lsblk pipeline libgtop ran on every refresh is gone.
  * Network: the headline counts physical interfaces only, so traffic through
    bridges, container and VPN interfaces is no longer counted twice.
  * New --version option.
  * GPU: NVIDIA figures come from NVML in-process instead of running
    nvidia-smi every second, and a powered-down GPU is shown as such and not
    woken to be asked. lscpu no longer runs at startup.
  * Fixes a crash when closing the main window, and a crash when an action
    dialog was opened while no window of the app was active.
  * New Health tab: storage wear and lifespan estimates from SMART data
    (udisks2 first, then a pkexec helper for what udisks cannot read), which
    processes write the most, battery wear, and an opt-in background sampler
    that keeps a local history. Estimated figures say so.
  * Process properties show the thread count and the kind of process (kernel
    thread, system or user); the process table has a Threads column.
  * Fixes memory growth in the process list, the user-name cache and the
    graph widgets over long sessions.
  * Release builds contain no developer control socket. The polkit policy and
    privileged helpers install under /usr, and the storage wear sampler's
    user unit under /usr/lib/systemd/user.
  * Processes: the list and its figures come from the monitor's own
    collector library by default instead of libgtop: about 39 system calls
    per shown process per refresh instead of about 90. CPU share is the
    scheduler's run time over wall time; disk write excludes writes later
    cancelled by truncation; "My processes" selects by real user, so the
    user's own non-dumpable processes (ssh-agent and the like) are no longer
    hidden. The gsettings key process-source switches back to libgtop.
  * Processes: a Class column puts every process in a workload class
    (kernel, init, system services, session services, applications, login
    session, agent sessions, containers and VMs, unclassified) by an ordered
    rule table over its cgroup, with the rule in the tooltip. The Unit column
    shows the innermost systemd unit (an application's own scope rather than
    user@1000.service). A Swap column is available. Unit, session and owner
    come from the cgroup path instead of a libsystemd call per process.
  * Processes: CPU share shows "—" when there is no rate yet (a process's
    first refresh, a failed read, a reused process id) instead of a wrapped
    value that read as 1600 %. Kernel threads' memory is 0, not unknown.
  * Resources: per-CPU graphs read /proc/stat by CPU number, so an offline
    CPU no longer shifts the others, and interrupt time counts as busy.
    Graph scales hold rates above 4 GiB/s, and the disk graph no longer
    rescales on every refresh.

